===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|unto}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*untō}}, {{m|ang|*und tō}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|un|t1=against; toward; up to|to}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|ont to}} {{gloss|"until"; > {{cog|stq|antou|t=until}}}} (cf. {{cog|ofs|und|t=up to; till}}, {{cog|ofs|til|t=till; to}}), {{cog|osx|untō}}, {{m|osx|untuo|t=until}}, {{cog|goh|unze}}, {{m|goh|unzi}}, {{m|goh|unza|t=until}}, {{cog|non|und|t=as far as; up to}}, {{cog|got|𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌴|t=until; as long as}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈʌntʊ/|/-tuː/|/-tə/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈʌntə/|/-tuː/|/-tʊ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-unto.ogg|Audio (GA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌntə|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|un|to}}

===Conjunction===
{{en-con}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|poetic}} Up to the [[time]] or [[degree]] that; [[until]].
#: {{w|Unto This Last}} ({{w|John Ruskin}})

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|till}}; see also [[Thesaurus:until]]

===Preposition===
{{en-prep}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} Up to, indicating a motion towards a thing and then stopping at it.
#: {{ux|en|Sir Gawain rode '''unto''' the nearby castle.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|I|ii|text=Come '''unto''' these yellow sands,<br>And then take hands;<br>Curtsied when you have, and kiss'd<br>The wild waves whist,<br>Foot it featly here and there,<br>And sweet sprites bear<br>The burthen.{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} [[to|To]], indicating an indirect object.
#: {{ux|en|And the Lord said '''unto''' Moses {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|IV|i|text=So please my lord the duke and all the court<br>To quit the fine for one half of his goods<br>I am content; so he will let me have<br>The other half in use, to render it,<br>Upon his death, '''unto''' the gentleman<br>That lately stole his daughter:<br>Two things provided more,—{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|passage=Again, whereas men affirm they perceive an addition of ponderosity in dead bodies, comparing them usually '''unto''' blocks and stones, whensoever they lift or carry them; this accessional preponderancy is rather in appearance than reality.}}

